WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.870
NO, I don’t think we can talk about the
Caribbean without mentioning the Dominican

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:05.870
Republic.

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:10.170
It’s major tourist destination with the
largest economy and even the highest mountain

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:20.750
of all the island countries in the Caribbean.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:23.960
The Dominican Republic is a very, very special
place in Latin America.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:28.240
And I’m not only saying this because we
can find amazing places such as Punta Cana,

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:31.260
Bayahibe or Puerto Plata here, among many
others.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:35.480
But also because this was the first place
where Christopher Columbus’s men settled

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:37.650
when America was discovered in 1492.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:38.820
Yes, it’s true.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:46.240
For good or ill, everything started here,
this is where Latin America, as we know it,

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:48.540
began.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:56.710
Its capital, Santo Domingo was the first modern
city on the entire American continent.

00:00:56.710 --> 00:01:00.460
Here the first cathedral, the first university
and the first hospital were built.

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:12.590
But... yes, it's true, if we associate the
Dominican Republic with anything, it's tourism.

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:20.080
(THE SUN REVOLUTION)

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.410
This is by far this country’s best face.

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:23.510
And not for nothing.

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:25.630
Folks, the Dominican Republic is trendy.

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:30.990
Thanks to its 1,000 miles of coastline, more
than 250 miles of amazing Caribbean beaches,

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:35.890
its golf courses and huge all-inclusive resorts,
the country is experiencing an impressive

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:39.640
tourist boom.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.770
Of course... this story has been developing
for a while.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:47.810
See, during the 40s, the then plenipotentiary
dictator of the island, General Rafael Trujillo

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:50.300
, gave the order to encourage tourism.

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:53.080
Probably, with the intention of filling up
his pockets.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:56.780
That’s how the Malecón de Santo Domingo
and the first luxury hotel in the city, the

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:59.640
Hotel Jaragua, opened in 1944.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:03.810
Years later, in 1968, the first legislation
that considered tourism development to be

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:05.530
of national interest was approved.

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:12.940
So as you can see, an emphasis on tourism
isn’t new here.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:14.610
But...

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:17.050
In recent years, it has completely taken off.

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:21.420
In 2017, the Dominican Republic, a country
with just over 10 million inhabitants, managed

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:26.940
to attract more than 7 million tourists.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:35.060
It grew so quickly that the country is expected
to overcome the 10 million international tourists

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:39.230
barrier in no more than 3 years.

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:43.510
And of course, as you can imagine all this
has consequences:

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:48.180
See, in the last 5 years direct foreign investment
in the tourism sector has grown by more than

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.200
500%, that is, it has multiplied by 6.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:55.650
Nowadays, the Dominican Republic has more
than 80,000 hotel rooms... and this number

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:57.470
won’t stop growing.

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:06.120
(“To give you an idea, to accommodate all
these visitors, in 2017 Dominican hotels opened

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:13.330
8,366 new rooms and an additional 15,000 are
under construction.”

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:16.350
Danilo Medina, President of the Dominican
Republic.)

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:17.350
The result?

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:21.220
Tourism is already the island’s main economic
activity, and if we take both the wealth this

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:27.060
generates directly and indirectly into account...
it means 1 out of every 4 pesos generated

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:28.340
by the economy.

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:29.340
25%!

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:32.890
We could say that the Dominican Republic has
discovered and is beginning to exploit its

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:34.580
peculiar oil.

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:38.360
And the truth is that, in this area, almost
all news is good.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:41.820
Check out one of their latest achievements...
which happened in exchange for breaking with

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:42.820
Taiwan.

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:47.790
("The People’s Republic of China is opening
up to Dominican tourism.

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:52.739
The Chinese authorities declare the Dominican
Republic as a tourist destination for its

00:03:52.739 --> 00:03:53.739
citizens” El Día.)

00:03:53.739 --> 00:03:56.000
Of course... just a moment… because that’s
not it.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.730
Folks, the Dominican Republic has a different
face.

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:03.660
Because here, in this country, we can find
a lot of light, yes… but also a lot of the

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:08.230
shadows that we commonly see today in Latin
America.

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:13.590
Let’s take a look.

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:18.570
(THE OTHER FACE)

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:22.620
The Dominican Republic has achieved one of
the highest growth rates in Latin America

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:25.060
and the Caribbean in the last 25 years.

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:28.999
This strong performance has actually meant
achieving a higher purchasing power parity,

00:04:28.999 --> 00:04:33.449
as well as one of the 10 highest per capita
incomes in all of Latin America.

00:04:33.449 --> 00:04:38.279
At almost $17,000, the Dominican Republic
is paired with Costa Rica and well above countries

00:04:38.279 --> 00:04:42.470
such as Peru, Colombia or Ecuador.

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:45.100
But wait a second because that’s not the
whole story.

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:50.569
In spite of everything, 32% of all Dominicans,
that is, one in three people, live below the

00:04:50.569 --> 00:04:55.189
poverty line; the average life expectancy
barely exceeds 70 and infant mortality is

00:04:55.189 --> 00:05:00.610
almost 5 times higher than in the developed
countries.

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:04.611
That may explain why we can find one and a
half million Dominicans in the United States...

00:05:04.611 --> 00:05:08.740
and why after tourism, remittances are the
second largest source of wealth.

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:12.800
But... having said that, I think the question
we can all ask ourselves is, how can such

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:20.199
a paradise not take off?

00:05:20.199 --> 00:05:24.580
Well folks… the answer is that this Caribbean
land has many of the "evils" that unfortunately

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:28.759
are all over Latin American.

00:05:28.759 --> 00:05:33.990
Yes, the continent’s modern history began
here in 1492.

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:40.919
Now, 526 years later, we can find a good reflection
of the best and worst in Latin America in

00:05:40.919 --> 00:05:42.150
this exact same place.

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:45.689
But... delving into the matter, the first
great point of darkness we can find is the

00:05:45.689 --> 00:05:50.710
stubborn persistence in keeping policies that
have repeatedly been shown not to work--an

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:53.779
issue that seems to reign in this region.

00:05:53.779 --> 00:05:58.199
For example, in matters of informal work--
irregular employment-- which is a serious

00:05:58.199 --> 00:06:01.749
problem that has spread throughout the region.

00:06:01.749 --> 00:06:08.559
See, the workers who are exposed to this situation,
to informality, work under more precarious

00:06:08.559 --> 00:06:14.009
conditions, with greater instability, problems
obtaining financing – be it to buy a house,

00:06:14.009 --> 00:06:19.219
invest in a business or to properly educate
their children – and of course lower wages.

00:06:19.219 --> 00:06:26.569
Because… obviously… a company will invest
much less money in an employee who’s hired

00:06:26.569 --> 00:06:27.569
irregularly.

00:06:27.569 --> 00:06:33.500
And this is also a problem for the government,
for example, when it comes to collecting taxes.

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:40.539
Well, in the Dominican Republic, informal
employment affects 6 out of every 10 workers.

00:06:40.539 --> 00:06:44.189
Because in this country, not only is that
number not decreasing, it’s growing.

00:06:44.189 --> 00:06:47.940
In 2017, for example, formal jobs were only
created in the public sector.

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:49.740
And what does this info tell us?

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:55.460
Well, that formal hiring involves many barriers
and burdens: bureaucracy, taxes, outdated

00:06:55.460 --> 00:07:00.389
regulations, that anyone setting up a business
has to pay taxes even before they make a single

00:07:00.389 --> 00:07:05.159
dollar, etc, etc...

00:07:05.159 --> 00:07:09.389
And all that, logically, makes it difficult
for private economies to create quality jobs...

00:07:09.389 --> 00:07:10.690
So with this data in hand...

00:07:10.690 --> 00:07:14.340
I think it would make sense to say… hey…
if we need to boost formal hiring, let’s

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:15.939
just change what isn’t working, right?

00:07:15.939 --> 00:07:17.639
Well, no, it doesn’t work like that.

00:07:17.639 --> 00:07:19.469
The same goes for commercial policy.

00:07:19.469 --> 00:07:24.280
See in a country with a small local market,
as is, precisely, the Dominican Republic’s

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:26.490
case, the foreign sector is essential.

00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:27.889
Obvious, right?

00:07:27.889 --> 00:07:33.469
Well, in this country we can basically find
two different situations.

00:07:33.469 --> 00:07:39.649
On one hand, there’s the free zones, where
the companies installed have favorable conditions,

00:07:39.649 --> 00:07:44.659
both regulatory and fiscal, which they provide
in exchange for basically only working in

00:07:44.659 --> 00:07:48.439
exports, while meeting certain criteria and
being approved by the government.

00:07:48.439 --> 00:07:53.429
On the other hand, there are the more normal
companies... which are the vast majority.

00:07:53.429 --> 00:07:56.869
Well, more than half of all exports come from
the free zones...

00:07:56.869 --> 00:08:00.779
And, additionally, in recent years exports
from these places have grown an average of

00:08:00.779 --> 00:08:05.110
more than 5% every year.

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:07.649
But exports from common Dominican companies...

00:08:07.649 --> 00:08:15.749
Well... check out this chart.

00:08:15.749 --> 00:08:20.139
So upon seeing this... the question I ask
myself is... if the free zones work…

00:08:20.139 --> 00:08:23.529
Why not extend their benefits to the rest
of the country so that Dominican companies

00:08:23.529 --> 00:08:24.779
can become more competitive?

00:08:24.779 --> 00:08:26.830
Well, no, that won’t happen either.

00:08:26.830 --> 00:08:31.389
And then... there’s the issue of deficit,
public spending and debt.... which in spite

00:08:31.389 --> 00:08:36.870
of the great number of bad experiences, continues
to be a vice of many Latin American governors.

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:39.820
Here, the Dominican Republic isn’t the exception
either.

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:43.340
Since 1990, public spending in the Dominican
Republic has multiplied... listen up… by

00:08:43.340 --> 00:08:44.340
18.

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:55.290
Here, public deficit is almost 
a tradition.

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:57.310
Now, just a moment...

00:08:57.310 --> 00:08:59.490
You may be wondering.. but…

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:03.520
Simon... we’re talking about a country that’s
coming out of poverty... there’s lots to

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:07.910
do… infrastructure needs to be created,
like ports, airports, roads, etc, etc... so

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:10.140
that the country can grow.

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:11.140
Well...

00:09:11.140 --> 00:09:12.970
Do you know what type of expenditure grows
the most?

00:09:12.970 --> 00:09:16.780
No, sorry, it isn’t public investment, it’s
current expenditure!

00:09:16.780 --> 00:09:19.210
Which has multiplied by almost 30 since 1990.

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:24.210
And of course... expenses have to be paid…
partly with more taxes, which slows down the

00:09:24.210 --> 00:09:27.960
creation of formal jobs and investment, and
partly with debt.

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:31.970
Check out what’s happening with the Dominican
debt... with debt and interests... because

00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:36.950
of course... debt has interests.

00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:40.800
Listen up.

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:42.730
Indeed.

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:46.510
Almost one in four euros that the government
spends goes towards interests....

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:50.610
It’s the third largest figure in all of
Latin America and it won’t stop growing.

00:09:50.610 --> 00:09:51.970
What a crazy business!

00:09:51.970 --> 00:09:56.470
Well, despite the tourism sector’s takeoff,
this constant stubbornness with policies that

00:09:56.470 --> 00:10:00.260
don’t work explains why Dominicans have
so many problems and why there’s still so

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:02.410
much poverty in the country.

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:09.050
Check out how this country ranks in the Global
Competitiveness Index.

00:10:09.050 --> 00:10:13.190
But...

00:10:13.190 --> 00:10:14.560
What about politicians?

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:16.680
Are they involved in other things?

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:28.220
Listen up, because the shadows don’t end
here... there’s more.

00:10:28.220 --> 00:10:30.300
(SUSPICIONS)

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:36.640
Folks, the Dominican Republic is a fantastic
country and it probably has a promising future...

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:41.320
And when it manages to take that big step…
it really will be like paradise...

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:43.680
Think about it: Good weather, good beaches
and money.

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:44.860
Who could resist?

00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:49.050
However, today the Dominican Republic has
two other very important problems that may

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:52.960
be stopping the country from taking off: the
first one is corruption.

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:59.420
(“Dominican Republic, among the 50 most
corrupt countries, according to Transparency

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:00.420
International” DiarioLibre)

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:04.440
You heard that right… the Dominican Republic
is considered one of the most corrupt countries

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:05.610
in the world.

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:10.160
According to Transparency International's
ranking, only Venezuela, Haiti, Guatemala

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:13.720
and Nicaragua are in a worse situation in
Latin America.

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:16.360
Here, corruption at all levels is very common...

00:11:16.360 --> 00:11:20.680
Suspects range from the current socialist
president, Danilo Medina, who is accused of

00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:25.380
having financed his electoral campaign with
bribes, to the police itself.

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:31.230
In fact, it was in the Dominican Republic
that the construction company Odebrecht installed

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:35.680
its coordination center for the bribes it
paid internationally in exchange for contracts.

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:39.970
And of course... many of these bribes remained
there, almost 100 million dollars.

00:11:39.970 --> 00:11:44.370
As far as we know, these were received by
politicians and officials in exchange for

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:47.900
concessions, work awards and public contracts.

00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:55.840
But unfortunately, that’s not all, the other
problem, which is growing... is drug trafficking.

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:59.860
The Dominican Republic is considered one of
the most important bridge counties and narcotics

00:11:59.860 --> 00:12:03.120
distribution centers by the United States.

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:07.550
( “Drug traffickers have a problem exporting
drugs from Venezuela.

00:12:07.550 --> 00:12:13.350
There are few commercial flights, little container
shipping, no tourists and a collapsed fishing

00:12:13.350 --> 00:12:14.350
sector.

00:12:14.350 --> 00:12:17.380
But the Dominican Republic, 1,400 kilometers
away, has it all”.

00:12:17.380 --> 00:12:18.380
InSight Crime)

00:12:18.380 --> 00:12:22.450
In fact, according to the DEA and the European
intelligence services, nowadays the Dominican

00:12:22.450 --> 00:12:26.821
Republic is one of the main transit countries
for narcotics that go to the United States

00:12:26.821 --> 00:12:29.870
and the most frequent for cocaine shipments
going to Europe.

00:12:29.870 --> 00:12:34.220
And obviously this is an important issue...
but we'll talk about that some other day.

00:12:34.220 --> 00:12:36.350
In any case, my friends, let's be clear.

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:40.980
These are problems that, unfortunately, are
common in almost the entire region.

00:12:40.980 --> 00:12:45.310
The Dominican Republic has achieved a lot
in recent decades, it’s a fantastic place

00:12:45.310 --> 00:12:47.160
and its future is promising.

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:50.810
In this video we wanted to show you some of
the reasons why this country isn’t doing

00:12:50.810 --> 00:12:52.030
as well as it should.

00:12:52.030 --> 00:12:55.490
